Q: Epoll trigger events associated with a file descriptor A: <p>The thing blocking you is that epoll has no &quot;get&quot; call. data.ptr is write-only from your side: epoll only hands it back inside epoll_wait, there's no epoll_ctl that says &quot;give me the event registered for this fd.&quot; So events_stop_fd, which only has the fd, can't recover the callback through epoll at all. That's not a gap in your code, the interface genuinely doesn't expose it.</p>
<p>So you do have to store the fd to cbk association somewhere. It just doesn't have to be a map. fds are always the lowest free non-negative integer, so they stay dense and small, and a plain vector indexed by fd is O(1) and about as cheap as it gets:</p>
<pre><code>static std::vector&lt;cbk_t&gt; cbks;   // indexed by fd

err_e events_add(event_t event, cbk_t cbk) {
    if ((int)cbks.size() &lt;= event.fd) cbks.resize(event.fd + 1, nullptr);
    cbks[event.fd] = cbk;
    struct epoll_event ev = { .events = event.events, .data = { .fd = event.fd } };
    return epoll_ctl(epoll_fd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, event.fd, &amp;ev) ? ERROR_NOTOK : ERROR_OK;
}
</code></pre>
<p>now stop_fd needs no lookup structure beyond that array:</p>
<pre><code>err_e events_stop_fd(int fd) {
    epoll_ctl(epoll_fd, EPOLL_CTL_DEL, fd, NULL);   // before close, DEL needs a live fd
    cbk_t cbk = cbks[fd];
    cbks[fd] = nullptr;
    if (cbk) cbk(ERROR_NOTOK);
    return ERROR_OK;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I moved the fd back into data.fd there since the array is the source of truth now. If you'd rather keep the callback in data.ptr for the wait path so you're not indexing on every event, do both, they don't conflict.</p>
<p>On your second idea, having events_wait do the call: there's no epoll call that synthesizes pending events, you can't ask the kernel to fire everything for fd N. What you can do is wake the loop yourself. Register an eventfd in the same epoll set, have events_stop_fd push the fd onto a small queue and write to the eventfd, and events_wait drains that queue when it wakes and does the DEL + callback from the loop thread. That's worth doing for real given the threading you described: you said callbacks get called from anywhere but waiting happens in one place. If stop_fd runs on another thread while events_wait is blocked, you've got two threads mutating the epoll set and the callback table at once. Funnelling the stop through the eventfd keeps all that on the one thread that owns the loop, and stop_fd is just enqueue + write.</p>
<p>For the single-threaded case the array plus the direct-call stop_fd above is all you need, and your main() works as written, the stop call fires the printf right away.</p>

Q: Having problems comparing two custom class objects A: <p>Though not an exact duplicate, you have all the answers here: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/104158/what-is-best-practice-for-comparing-two-instances-of-a-reference-type">What is "Best Practice" For Comparing Two Instances of a Reference Type?</a>. Get the snippet provided Konrad's answer from that thread to never get it wrong. </p>

<hr>

<p>In short, you are doing it wrong. You're not comparing the actual values in your generic <code>Equals</code> method. Furthermore its not safe to call <code>==</code> operator inside <code>Equals</code> methods. Do it like this:</p>

<pre><code>public override int GetHashCode()
{
    return pType.GetHashCode() ^ pSubType.GetHashCode() ^ quantityDiscountsDT.GetHashCode();
    //or something similar, but fast.
}

public bool Equals(QuantityBasedDiscount other)
{
   if (ReferenceEquals(null, other))
    {
        return false;
    }

    if (ReferenceEquals(this, other))
    {
        return true;
    }

    return pType == other.pType &amp;&amp; pSubType == other.pSubType &amp;&amp; 
           quantityDiscountsDT == other.quantityDiscountsDT;
}
</code></pre>

<p>This might still give you inconsistent results if your DataTable field is modified. All that depends on how <code>==</code> operator is implemented for a DataTable. To have more control on that you will have to derive your own DataTable..</p>

<p>Additionally you might want to overload <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> operator as well. For all that check the provided link. </p>
